on" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1+(-2)\\text{i}=1-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0+1\\text{i} \\\\ 2+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-38569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers