ion/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & -7\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-7$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-7+(-2)\\text{i}=-7-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+(-2)\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-2\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-39132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-39132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-39132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$\\lambda =-2∓7\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =-7\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2∓\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =2∓7\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-39132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-140494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-140494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-140494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-140494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-140494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-140494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-140496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7+4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-140496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-140496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-140496" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathac