e/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=7\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-99419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=4\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-99411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2-3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-2+3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-99418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+2\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+2\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-4+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-4+2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-38562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(2-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-99469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1-2\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -13 & -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -13{x}_{1}+(-3+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-3-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-3-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{13}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-99480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{26}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{13}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-99480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{13}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-2\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+3\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-38565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-38565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-39143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3+2\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-39143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3+2\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -10{x}_{1}+(-6-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-39143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7166ee5fdb9a55a873b7fd8244d7357cc9a484e35e7df5db86fc0dfeea5b7d52"} {"question" "q-39143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-39143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "q-39143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/state.json" "file_sha256" "810646eb602bf82225fb1fc6de7d72e6a4b9ce87ad8ea9dbaa64046422b261e8"} {"question" "e-5531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/d1bc2b703c1d73e735bdbb1d2abab86f86447e87fbeabde57126114726f78757.png)\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PCP^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/example-5531.json" "file_sha256" "a6034edd05431a945a13a868a147f2a54126281ac9e3f04f310407e82ea0acbb"} {"question" "e-5531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/558cbc7aba862d44e34a7b109ea84c1f7a6f3f1f4b505e5f476e62a29fdd654b.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\n- From the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ca2243ed8015927b9db5428d676ee59907264fc223721246a81b2e73aca3b351.png) we deduce that $\\textrm{Re}(\\lambda)={\\color{blue}3}$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\lambda) = {\\color{red}-1}.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = {\\color{blue}3}+({\\color{red}-1})\\text{i} = 3-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/7a5bb57b71eb218271f2bb0ab718304d6b834738f1004eac7c501a7fd4ffa2b3.png) we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/0ea51255e7594236cdd5d8dee0997f64b49f77cbb77e8cfd97937830a862283b.png)\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ff8f2c8cace2b5c8c2c2ce2fcd0d5c24a4d1f4692e8e06464a9ae4666d429224.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/example-5531.json" "file_sha256" "a6034edd05431a945a13a868a147f2a54126281ac9e3f04f310407e82ea0acbb"} {"question" "e-5530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/5f3cd6aeb514ee0a59d1f3534342f85e1c00743d9e7c7004b8aaf8ccfbe353f9.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1-4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/example-5530.json" "file_sha256" "76b3e8ced4991a20ecef4bd3e6f28f36d01282ff7cb55f4316e012d9777bf686"} {"question" "e-5530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/9fb15e52a1df912c9e5fb4238f45d705cd0a56fb7190620c0f0fd597f9a2b8c0.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/80bd36915851e6ebeb67befe8af41e5496fa5491b9d87c294d8b937eb586319f.png)\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/example-5530.json" "file_sha256" "76b3e8ced4991a20ecef4bd3e6f28f36d01282ff7cb55f4316e012d9777bf686"} {"question" "e-8734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/f4a051da70308d92a5530fca3ba9c214cff919a6072e0e4d863f5aad10f405ee.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2+4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/example-8734.json" "file_sha256" "282519dc625747956db79006652afdda37fefb0f9415e8ddcc60699dfdeead27"} {"question" "e-8734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/4a53548a17d9b4fd870ce70285bfc2c57cf6c8d352f75149e5aa7511ef4af853.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/aa5738b600440bf8327670fc21cd8005c660def232119b2b4808237d988367bd.png)\n\nwhile\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/fc91fcabd47720075061e670f62be9ea9b636070a05cba70752bb7060608b764.png)\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda.$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}},$ the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/example-8734.json" "file_sha256" "282519dc625747956db79006652afdda37fefb0f9415e8ddcc60699dfdeead27"} {"question" "e-5532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/74cc68e763183d4599dc223cce44849ace88129bbc82b6d49bcbafc4a1a8163d.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i},$ and that $A=PCP^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, find the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/example-5532.json" "file_sha256" "fad3c42bf2f1c71d420c9febb12be1b73fc2e96d0f9e43d54eb34bc745316d05"} {"question" "e-5532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/b5c1766ea1d9eb7a913fd7be2d7bf71909c81da03aaff8f4c631e1a3955f9589.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/7f29c1c9be88268b14ef9131599420874fff6a399a3e3c92d3f4b635906163f6.png)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions to the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =-2+6\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+6\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3-6\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 9 & -3-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3-6\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+(-3-6\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3(1+2\\textrm{i})x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ab958a638d0bce3cb2498d6f64016e581bb303fd0df725abcfaa58dbd836b95d.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=3$, we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/631f718ed2642536c5a8e02dedc38544af5e8b6cc48bff7a0afcf77c0d057d1b.png)\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/example-5532.json" "file_sha256" "fad3c42bf2f1c71d420c9febb12be1b73fc2e96d0f9e43d54eb34bc745316d05"} {"question" "q-39130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$\\lambda =5\\mp 2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "4302c0277724df3241576e6bed98ade7bbef7461e1234bc476a3fb0eec970861" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "The original question and solution screenshots show λ=5∓2i paired with v=(-1±4i,3). With p1=(-1,3) and p2=(4,0), C(1,i)=(5-2i)(1,i), so A(p1+i p2)=(5-2i)(p1+i p2). Conjugation gives eigenvalue 5+2i with vector p1-i p2. Since det(P)=-12, these are valid eigenpairs of A=PCP^-1. Thus original option a (index 0) is correct. Both captured text and stored text dropped the upper stroke of ∓, yielding a plain minus; the derived option value must restore that source-visible symbol. Saved Incorrect grading concerns submitted option e, as the solution screenshot shows, and does not contradict option a." "bindings" {"problem" "f9c17c728d2cb9b6df63da7743c42c020c68c2ae2056f09a04b0de7dd5ca5a5d" "worked_solution" "42e8b71193943ea17b16a369438bd3cc60c741eedc6eadc35470a9adaa00a943" "original_field" "ea41da9309aac186cbcdcf2b37e36f4f2007f7407a30a682aef337906a5f2e18" "reviewed_field" "f356a8bd4d4806a6eef54829a6027829a5ed2e298de26aedc5e86cc1f25a7dc1" "raw_widget" "750ed910b7679c8da9b520044e7b715949c86cd258b61d36baf34dd3db34b196"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-39130-before.png" "sha256" "2ecd46faa7064408ea2326089f390f57d3ab8d47679f0bf702c34d387862ff3e"} {"path" "q-39130-before.json" "sha256" "181e54691f04edfd16055cb99667570a61de06d2b947f21225fd923026a8f348"} {"path" "history-q-39130.png" "sha256" "1b7cc73b1e11c6cd1a3eb5d6701440539163c1ed4baf9c299a757958ec649c45"} {"path" "q-39130-after.json" "sha256" "73580366844b38b54f3f601c49e0529baf2df192b65dd1db082ff50a26d44ad0"}]} {"question" "q-39130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$\\lambda =-1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =-1∓4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =2\\pm 5\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =5\\mp 2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =5∓2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "4302c0277724df3241576e6bed98ade7bbef7461e1234bc476a3fb0eec970861" "rationale" "The original question and solution screenshots show λ=5∓2i paired with v=(-1±4i,3). With p1=(-1,3) and p2=(4,0), C(1,i)=(5-2i)(1,i), so A(p1+i p2)=(5-2i)(p1+i p2). Conjugation gives eigenvalue 5+2i with vector p1-i p2. Since det(P)=-12, these are valid eigenpairs of A=PCP^-1. Thus original option a (index 0) is correct. Both captured text and stored text dropped the upper stroke of ∓, yielding a plain minus; the derived option value must restore that source-visible symbol. Saved Incorrect grading concerns submitted option e, as the solution screenshot shows, and does not contradict option a."}] "usage" {"q-38557" [] "q-38565" [] "e-5530" [] "q-39130" [] "e-5531" [] "e-8734" [] "e-5532" []} "basis" 2569 "retractions" [[17592186129651 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A = PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"] [17592186129651 :question/answer-fields 17592186127700] [17592186129601 :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 3 + 4\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A = PCP^{-1}$."] [17592186129603 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A = PCP^{-1}$, where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of\n$\\frac{a^{2} + b^{2}}{c^{2} + d^{2}}$?"] [17592186128883 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"] [17592186128883 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda$.\n\n- From the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ - (-1) & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we deduce that $\\textrm{Re}(\\lambda)={\\color{blue}3}$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\lambda) = {\\color{red}-1}$. So, our eigenvalue is\n$\\lambda = {\\color{blue}3}+({\\color{red}-1})\\text{i} = 3-\\text{i}$.\n- From the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-1)\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 + 0\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda$, and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}$.\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 ± \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186128882 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 + 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1-4\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}$."] [17592186128882 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Re(λ) & Im(λ) \\\\ -Im(λ) & Re(λ)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 + 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Re(v)) + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Im(v))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= Re(v) \\mid Im(v) \\\\\n&= \\mid \\mid \\mid ] = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128968 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2+4\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}$."] [17592186128968 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Re(λ) & Im(λ) \\\\ -Im(λ) & Re(λ)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Re(λ^(―)) & Im(λ^(―)) \\\\ -Im(λ^(―)) & Re(λ^(―))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe complex conjugate of $\\lambda$. Hence, to construct the matrix $P$, we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}}$, the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}$.\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv^(―) &= \\begin{bmatrix}2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Re(v^(―))) + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Im(v^(―)))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= Re(v^(―)) \\mid Im(v^(―)) \\\\\n&= \\mid \\mid \\mid ] = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128884 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i}$, and that $A=PCP^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, find the matrix $P$."] [17592186128884 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda$.\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions to the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_1 =-2+6\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-2 + 6\\textrm{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 + 6\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 - 6\\textrm{i} & -5 \\\\ 9 & -3 - 6\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{(3 - 6\\textrm{i})x_{1} - 5x_{2} = 0; 9x_{1} + (-3 - 6\\textrm{i})x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3(1+2\\textrm{i})x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}(1 + 2\\textrm{i})x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_2=3$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nP = [ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ] = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127401 17592186127402 17592186127403 17592186127404 17592186127405 17592186127413 17592186127414 17592186127415 17592186127416 17592186127417 17592186127701 17592186127702 17592186127703 17592186127704 17592186127705] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127400 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127400 :answer-field/id] [17592186127400 :answer-field/key] [17592186127400 :answer-field/type] [17592186127412 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127412 :answer-field/id] [17592186127412 :answer-field/key] [17592186127412 :answer-field/type] [17592186127700 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127700 :answer-field/id] [17592186127700 :answer-field/key] [17592186127700 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
